{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>The birthday of Pharaoh.</b> On that day a son was born to the king and they called his name Pharaoh after the name of his father, and their custom was to make a feast every year on that day of birth." "<b>And he lifted up the head of the chief cupbearer and the head of the chief baker among his servants.</b> He appointed these two eunuchs among the rest of his servants; and the expression וישא is from the language of (Exodus 30:12) ״כי תשא את ראש״. And since he mentioned them at the feast among the rest, he intended to judge them and bring their sentence to light: one emerged innocent and was restored to his rank, and the second was found liable and was hanged." "<b>And there is</b> in this matter a great awakening for human beings, that all their deeds are weighed according to punishment and reward, and they are destined to give a judgment and reckoning before Him who spoke and the world came to be." "<b>And the matter of</b> this perashah comes to teach us that it is fitting for a person to seek the king's favor and to be wary of his wrath. And so King Solomon, peace be upon him, admonished a person to strive after the king's favor and that the king's brightened countenance should be his, as he said (Proverbs 16:15) ״באור פני מלך חיים ורצונו כעב מלקוש״, and that he should be careful to keep far from his wrath so as not to provoke him, which is what he said (ibid.) ״חמת מלך מלאכי מות״. And it is written (ibid. 19) ״נהם ככפיר זעף מלך וכטל על עשב רצונו״ — he likened his favor and the brightening of his countenance to dew, which causes plants to bloom and grow, and they are sustenance for living creatures; and in order to teach you how severe is his wrath, he likened it to a lion, which destroys and consumes the living creatures that eat the plants. And Solomon's intent in all this is that a person should reason a fortiori within himself: if one who sins against a king of flesh and blood and provokes him — his wrath is bad and bitter and harsh to such a degree, then how much more so one who provokes the King of the king of kings, the Holy One, blessed be He, by his sins — all the more so. And therefore a person is obligated, by way of reason, to apply his mind wisely to secure His favor and to fear His wrath, as it is written (Psalms 30:6) ״כי רגע באפו חיים ברצונו״. For His favor and His wrath are the utmost of good and evil; and if one must guard against the wrath of a king of flesh and blood, who is likened to a lion, and must secure and seek his favor, which is likened to dew, then how much more so the King of the king of kings, the Holy One, blessed be He — whom Scripture likened to a lion, as it is said (Amos 3:8) ״אריה שאג מי לא יירא״ — and likened His favor to dew, as it is written (Hosea 14:6) ״אהיה כטל לישראל יפרח כשושנה ויך שרשיו כלבנון״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {3 "The abbreviation ממ״ה resolved as מלך מלך ה — 'King of the king of kings' — following the parallel structure with the earlier מלך בשר ודם / מלך מלכי המלכים; the abbreviation is slightly irregular and the resolution is inferential."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:19:18.024213458Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}